<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP is refused app-wide. There are no per-domain cleartext exceptions.

  Why no LAN exception, given that self-hosted Plex is often plain http:// on a LAN:

  Plex issues a wildcard certificate for *.plex.direct and publishes DNS that maps a
  hyphenated-IP subdomain to the address behind it, so a server on 192.168.1.7 is
  reachable as https://192-168-1-7.<hash>.plex.direct with a valid certificate. HTTPS on
  the local network is therefore genuinely available, not a compromise — which removes
  the reason to allow cleartext at all.

  Why an exception could not have been scoped anyway: Android matches <domain> by exact
  string or dot-boundary suffix (ApplicationConfig.getConfigForHostname). It does not
  parse CIDR or numeric ranges, so "10.0.0.0/8" is treated as a literal hostname and
  matches nothing — it would sit in this file looking like a LAN allowance while
  permitting exactly nothing. LAN addresses are DHCP-assigned and cannot be enumerated
  at build time, so no correct static list exists.

  Consequence, stated plainly: a server with Secure Connections set to "Disabled" will
  no longer connect over its LAN address. That is deliberate — the fix is to enable
  secure connections on the server, which Plex defaults to. See cu-42.

  Loopback is not listed here on purpose. The platform grants localhost its own config
  (ApplicationConfig.mLocalhostConfig), but only behind the networkSecurityConfigLocalhost
  flag on recent releases, so the debug override declares 127.0.0.1 explicitly for the
  mock Plex server rather than depending on it.
-->
<network-security-config>
  <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
    <trust-anchors>
      <certificates src="system" />
    </trust-anchors>
  </base-config>
</network-security-config>
